        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Sam Sip',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Sam Sip (Pa-Som Sip) is a draw-and-discard game from Thailand and Burma. Players draw from the stock or the previous player\'s discard, then discard one card. The aim is to be the first to hold three pairs: two cards totalling 10 (Ace=1), two 10s, or two equal picture cards (J, Q, K). Wild cards (jokers and cards matching the turned-up rank) can substitute for any card. The Thai name means "mixing tens".',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: Thailand and Burma\nTime Period: Traditional; first Western description from Phuket area, May 2001\nHistorical Development: Draw and discard game; Thai name means "mixing tens"; played on islands off Phuket and in Burma\nCultural Significance: Popular social and family game in Thailand and Burma',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 2\nMaximum Players: 6\nOptimal Players: 4 or 5\nPartnerships: Optional',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: Standard 52-card deck plus three jokers (55 cards) in Phuket version; some variants use 52 cards without jokers.\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Three in main version; none in Korat, Bangkok, Burma variants',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck (and three jokers for main version)\nTable space; stakes agreed in advance if playing for money',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards per Player: Five each\nDealing Pattern: Counter-clockwise, one card at a time\nOne card turned face up after the deal (wild indicator); remainder forms stock',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
